{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-344119",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{′}(5),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=5.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in [4,6].$ Notice that $x=5$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252554/assets/42b4ddb29283047bd7ea8141ee14c793607b6b79a18f5d599a93839aec5a77b5.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (4,6)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{5-(-1)}{6-4}=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(5)=3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
